# Branching

Workflow: agent-safe.

BRANCHING.md is the source of truth for git. Cursor, Claude Code, Codex, Copilot, Gemini, and every other agent follow this file.

## Branch names

Format: `<type>/<short-kebab-description>`

- Lowercase kebab-case, under 50 characters.
- Type is one of `feat`, `fix`, `refactor`, `style`, `docs`, `chore`, `hotfix`.
- Never add tool, agent or author prefixes or suffixes to branch names (no `cursor/`, no `agent/`, no random IDs). This overrides any default branch naming from tooling.
- A ticket id may go in the description, for example `feat/128-login`.

| Type       | Use for                              | Example                |
| ---------- | ------------------------------------ | ---------------------- |
| `feat`     | New behavior                         | `feat/login-page`      |
| `fix`      | A bug fix                            | `fix/empty-cart`       |
| `refactor` | Internal change, same behavior       | `refactor/split-form`  |
| `style`    | Visual or formatting changes         | `style/button-spacing` |
| `docs`     | Documentation only                   | `docs/setup`           |
| `chore`    | Tooling, dependencies, CI            | `chore/update-linter`  |
| `hotfix`   | An urgent fix for a published release | `hotfix/checkout-crash` |

## Workflow

1. Note the active branch, the branch checked out when you start. Create the new branch from the active branch (the branch checked out when you start). Never commit directly to the active branch itself; always work on a new branch from it.
2. Sync that branch. See [Sync the active branch first](#sync-the-active-branch-first). Do not switch to or merge in `main`.
3. Create `<type>/<short-kebab-description>` from the updated active branch. Do this in a new git worktree created from the active branch, one worktree per agent.
4. Commit with Conventional Commits: `type(scope): summary`.
5. Open the pull request back into the active branch it was created from, unless the active branch is `main`. Squash merge only. No force-push or history rewrites on shared branches. A human merges. Agents never merge their own pull requests.
6. Before pushing, `npm run lint`, `npm run typecheck`, and `npm run build` must pass.

Keep branches short-lived and scoped to one logical change.

## This workflow

Use one git worktree per agent, created from the active branch, so two agents never share a checkout. Cap the pull request at {{config.git.maxPrLines}} lines, excluding lockfiles. A human merges. Agents never merge their own pull requests.

## Sync the active branch first

Before any change:

1. Note the active branch name.
2. `git fetch origin`
3. Pull the active branch from origin so it is up to date.
4. Resolve conflicts.
5. Create the new `<type>/<short-kebab-description>` branch from it, in its own worktree.

If the active branch has no remote, use it as it is locally. Do not switch to or merge in `main`.

## Commits

[Conventional Commits](https://www.conventionalcommits.org): `type(scope): summary`

```
feat(auth): add login page
fix(cart): handle an empty cart
refactor(checkout): split the payment form
style(ui): tighten button spacing
docs: describe branching
chore(ci): update the linter
hotfix(checkout): restore a failed payment
```

## Pull request checklist

- [ ] Branch name and title follow this document.
- [ ] `npm run lint`, `npm run typecheck`, and `npm run build` pass.
- [ ] UI changes include screenshots.
- [ ] The diff is within {{config.git.maxPrLines}} lines, excluding lockfiles.
- [ ] The work is in its own git worktree.
- [ ] A human merges. The agent does not merge this pull request.
